<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arch of Germanicus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 18–19 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> is a Roman double arch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintes">Saintes</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, built around 18–19 CE at the entrance to a bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charente">Charente</a>. An inscription records that it was dedicated by Gaius Julius Rufus, a local notable of Santonian descent, to the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, his son Drusus and his adopted son Germanicus. When the old bridge was demolished in 1843, the arch was dismantled and rebuilt on the river bank.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arch-of-germanicus.jpg" alt="Arch of Germanicus" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Propre travail · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arc_de_Germanicus.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
